NAME
       awk - pattern scanning and processing language

SYNOPSIS
       awk [-F sep] 'program' [file]

DESCRIPTION
       Splits each input line into fields and runs the program against
       it. A program is a list of pattern { action } rules; a rule with
       no pattern runs on every line, and a rule with no action prints
       the line.

       Fields are $1, $2 and so on, $0 is the whole line. NR holds the
       current record number and NF the field count. length() is
       available, and print and printf write output.

OPTIONS
       -F S   use S as the field separator instead of whitespace

EXAMPLES
       awk '{ print $1 }' access.log
       awk -F: '$3 > 100 { print $1 }' /etc/passwd
       awk 'END { print NR }' file

NOTES
       Quote the program in single quotes. csh(1) does not expand $ in
       single quotes, so $1 reaches awk intact.

SEE ALSO
       sed(1), cut(1), grep(1)
